n runs is a symptom to chase, not noise.\n\n**2026-10-10: REACHED 1000/1094 on the nuc (ae03161c, 948 -\u003e 1000 over 553c9f70..ae03161c).** Method that worked: read-only triage agents (no builds, in-repo binary path, own WORK dir) fan out over row groups and return root cause + diff; I apply, build once, run the rows with `CASE_FILTER=... WORK=/tmp/rowsw haxe-conformance/run.sh`, then gate per batch and commit by whole files.\n- Linux-only: the untyped array formatter treated only addresses \u003c 16 TiB as heap pointers, so on x86-64 Linux every `Array\u003cString\u003e` printed integers (Mac heap is lower). Bound is now 2^47.\n- Switch cases leaked symbol_map bindings: a `for` in one case rebound a captured local function to a phi register the next case used (\"Function pointer IrId not found in value_map\", the haxe.macro.Printer lambda). Every case now starts from the pre-switch map.\n- Static reflection on class values: getter/setter thunks per static registered as `haxe_register_static_field`; a class value is a bare 32-bit id or a box whose payload == tag.\n- Traps I hit: `pgrep -f` matched my own ssh command (kills the session); a stdlib-wide guard (static through abstract instance) broke `haxe.Int64` compiling -\u003e every row; the LIB unit test `mapping_snapshot_unchanged` pins runtime_mapping (edit mapping_snapshot.txt with the change); \"integration passed: 87\" = a lib test failed, not a crash.\n- Not done (need user input): Issue10867 needs `fancy-regex` (new runtime dependency) for lookaround/backrefs.\n\n**2026-10-10 continued past 1000 (1002 -\u003e 1026+, commits 9688c093..batch 25, unpushed after ae03161c until the user says push).**\n- ASLR-DEPENDENT HANG: a row timed out in the harness but ran fine under gdb — gdb DISABLES ASLR. Use `gdb -ex \"set disable-randomization off\"`; `setarch -R` reproduces gdb's layout. ptrace attach is blocked (yama=1), so run the binary under gdb. The cause was `rayzor_anon_get_field` walking a non-anon object (an Array behind a structure type); anon handles are now tagged `AnonHandle{tag,arc}` + `is_anon_handle()`.\n- A MOVED row needs a HEAD A/B: reverse-apply the batch diff on the nuc (`git apply -R`), build, run, re-apply — no stash on the Mac needed.\n- The suite's 202/5/1 vs baseline 202/4/2 is `nue_smoke_llama_chat` flipping CRASH\u003c-\u003eFAIL; rerun the suite to confirm before blaming the batch.\n- PosInfos: NOTHING filled `?p:haxe.PosInfos` before batch 24; now synthesized at the call site (also abstract operator methods, @:from/@:to). The unit suite stages under src/ like upstream (`-cp src`) so fileName matches.\n- Commit trailers need the FULL 64-char git-bug id — I wrote short ids once and had to amend.\n- Typing changes (Null\u003cT\u003e for defaulted params, branch unification to a common base) go in their own batch and need the CI Benchmarks check.\n\n**2026-10-11: 1077/1092 pushed (dc81ea45). Goal now 100% (user).** Landed since 1063: Null\u003cInt\u003e stage 3 (inline Array\u003cNull\u003cInt\u003e\u003e), callee-side Int defaults (NullInt ABI, TAST type stays Int — Issue6972 checks it), function-type labels, code-point strings (+ nue tokenizers moved to Bytes, byte-identical), module-level fields as statics of a synthetic class placed FIRST, monomorph macro views, tag-only enum null = NULL_INT, Array\u003cInt\u003e-into-Array\u003cAny\u003e int flag.\n- Gate traps this round: the NUC also runs another project's builds (`ash`) — OOM-kills our rustc (\"could not compile ... (lib test)\", SIGKILL in the full log); wait for `cargo test --release -p ash` to finish, never touch it. A killed perf bisect left a STALE rayzor binary that agents probed for hours — rebuild + check `strings rayzor | grep RAYZOR_NULL_INT_BOXED` style markers.\n- `??` precedence: Haxe level 6 (tighter than comparisons and ||, left-assoc); a \"passing\" row depended on the old wrong precedence + a truth_of bug.\n- A callee-side default must be evaluated lazily (branch, not select)